  "summary": "The instrument works: one harness drives four arms over one frozen trace at matched durability, each engine in its own 128 MiB cgroup, and the inventory oracle passes everywhere it was run except the one flashdb case disclosed. At the pre-registered regime (flashdb 1.68 GB = 12.55x of the cap, one seed, the 1x rung) T5 passes by an enormous margin - flashdb-declared 49.95 rps against sqlite's and postgres's 50.00, a deficit of 1.001 against the 1.5x line - and T7 with it; but the same run shows both baselines SUSTAINING the rung with zero rejected windows (lag p99 6.1 ms and 2.2 ms) while flashdb-declared fails the sustained rule on one window of twenty (12.5 ms), so T5's number is evidence that flashdb's throughput is not behind, not that it is as well behaved. In the warm 0.14x ladder the ordering reverses and flashdb is not the fastest arm above 100 rps (sqlite sustains 200 cleanly, both flashdb arms are rejected at 200, T6 there 2.0x against the 3x line). T6 at the pre-registered regime - the actual external-superiority claim - remains unevaluated, and no result here supports a claim that flashdb beats postgres or sqlite at scale, a claim about the learned arm (in M1 it is the declared wiring), or a claim about drift and reorg cost.",
